      "id": "bbc-2026-07-15-money-box",
      "title": "Money Box",
      "summary": "Home News Sport Weather iPlayer Sounds Bitesize CBeebies CBBC Food Home News Sport Business Technology Health Culture Arts Travel Earth Audio Video Live Use BBC.com or the new BBC App to listen to BBC podcasts, Radio 4 and the World Service outside the UK.Find out how to listen to other BBC stationsEpisode detailsRadio 4,·15 Jul 2026,·28 minsMoney Box Live: Buy Now, Pay Later S…",
      "body": "Home            News            Sport            Weather            iPlayer            Sounds            Bitesize     CBeebies     CBBC     Food         Home     News     Sport     Business     Technology     Health     Culture     Arts     Travel     Earth     Audio     Video     Live          Use BBC.com or the new BBC App to listen to BBC podcasts, Radio 4 and the World Service outside the UK.Find out how to listen to other BBC stationsEpisode detailsRadio 4,·15 Jul 2026,·28 minsMoney Box Live: Buy Now, Pay Later Shake-UpMoney BoxAvailable for over a yearNew regulations begin today (Wednesday July 15th) for shoppers using Buy Now Pay Later. Consumers using services like Klarna and Clearpay will now be given more protection but also a greater risk of rejection, as new affordability checks begin.\nBNPL schemes often provide interest free payment options over several months and have proved immensely popular.  Experian estimates that a 100mn transactions were made that way last year, adding up to about £7bn.\nMeanwhile, new data from one of the country’s largest free money advice services, Money Wellness, shows BNPL has become an increasingly common feature of customers' finances over the past four years, with June recording 4,520 customers seeking help with BNPL debt.  That's the highest June on record and second only to the traditional post-Christmas spike seen in January.\nPresenter Felicity Hannah talks to the Financial Conduct Authority to find out if the new rules are going to improve things for shoppers.  She’s joined by an expert panel answering listener questions –  Mick McAteer of The Financial Inclusion Centre, a UK not-for-profit policy and research group. and Matthew Sheeran from Money Wellness.\nSenior News Editor: Sara Wadeson\nPhoto credit: Pakorn Supajitsoontorn via Getty Images.\n(This episode of Money Box Live was first broadcast on BBC Radio Four at 3pm on Wednesday 15th July)Programme WebsiteMore episodes",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-15T14:30:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/images/ic/1024x576/p0nypjz0.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sounds/play/m002yx7t?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
