      "id": "hackernews-2026-07-14-openai-s-ad-business-is-on-pace-to-miss-its-own-forecast-by-",
      "title": "OpenAI's Ad Business Is on Pace to Miss Its Own Forecast by 90%, Analyst Says",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.adweek.com/media/openais-ad-business-is-on-pace-to-miss-its-own-forecast-by-90-analyst-says/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48902599\nPoints: 30\n# Comments: 13",
      "body": "OpenAI’s ad business is on pace to fall short of the company’s own five-year revenue forecast by 90%, according to Emarketer.&nbsp;\nThe AI lab has projected $2.5 billion in ad revenue this year and $100 billion by 2030. Emarketer’s data finds that standalone chatbots like ChatGPT, Microsoft Copilot app, Google AI Mode, and Amazon Alexa for Shopping (formerly Rufus) in U.S, will generate less than $1 billion in ad revenue this year, and just $5.41 billion by 2030.\nOpenAI began its ad trial in February. Two months in, the AI lab made headlines touting those same projections.&nbsp;\nThe forecast assumes OpenAI captures search ad budgets en masse from traditional search ad sellers, dominates a fully mature chatbot ad market, and outperforms every ad format in history--all at once.&nbsp;\n“If you're willing to accept all those assumptions, then I can see their reported forecasts being true,” said Nate Elliot, principal analyst, AI, Emarketer. “But we're not willing to accept any of those assumptions.”\nAd spend inside AI assistants like ChatGPT will account for just 8% of the total digital ad spend this year, according to Emarketer data. Total digital ad spend will reach $32.03 billion in 2026, more than doubling to $68.25 billion in 2030.&nbsp;\nMost of that AI-linked spend is barely going to chatbots at all. More than 80% of AI advertising in 2026 will appear next to AI content, like traditional search ads running alongside Google’s AI Overviews, generating $26.42 billion in the U.S this year alone. Even as growth slows, that category will still represent 58.6% of AI ad spending in 2030.&nbsp;\n“Even if you assume that they will make 100% of all revenue from chatbot advertising five years from now, OpenAI will still miss their number by 90%,” Elliot said.&nbsp;\nHitting that target requires growth across three separate variables at once, according Matt Prohaska, ceo and principal of Prohaska Consulting: total audience size, average revenue per user, and the ad products themselves. OpenAI's recent push to court small businesses is one attempt to grow some of that pie.\nBut \"it's not like small business ad spend is going to be up 25% a year,\" Prohaska said. \"That TAM is a little more limited.\"\nThe dizzying numbers come ahead of the much anticipated IPO which the company filed last month.&nbsp;\n“If you look at the total addressable market OpenAI has put in their filings—the trillions they think they're going to be taking from other places or creating—obviously they have to throw a number that's huge to justify a trillion-dollar valuation for an IPO that is smartly being delayed right now,” Prohaska said.&nbsp;\nOpenAI did not respond to media request.\nHow OpenAI built its ad business in five months\nThe ChatGPT ad pilot formally began on February 9 this year, launching in the U.S with a minimum spend commitment as high as $250,000. By March, OpenAI began testing an ads manager with a small group of advertisers.&nbsp;\nIn comparison, it took Netflix about two years to launch a self-serve platform after starting its ad business, and Meta about three years.\nThat pilot crossed the $100 million annualized revenue mark within six weeks of launch–the same day OpenAI announced expansion to Canada, Australia, and New Zealand. At the same time, the company named David Dugan, a longtime ad executive at Meta, as its vp and head of global ads solutions.&nbsp;\nBy April, OpenAI quietly dropped its minimum spend threshold to $50,000, ADWEEK previously reported, widening the funnel to tap a broader pool of advertisers.&nbsp;\nAccording to Kevin Roy, founder of GreenBanana SEO, who has been testing ads on ChatGPT since June for his own company as well for fewer than a dozen clients, the highest anyone is spending is $5,000.&nbsp;\nOpenAI is also inching towards a more sophisticated ad product. According to Juozas Kaziukėnas, advisor at ecommerce ad platform Quartile, the company is rolling out conversion-optimized ads, or oCPC on ads manager. Kaziukėnas first saw the support for this infrastructure roughly a month ago, and as recently as last week, an update to the UI that supports it.&nbsp;\nThis new capability allows advertisers to adjust bids real-time based on the predicted likelihood that a given click will convert into sale. “If you are an ecommerce store, you are buying ads to drive sales. The smarter conversion optimized ad strategy works better for you because OpenAI’s ad platform is optimizing for the outcome you want,” Kaziukėnas said.&nbsp;\nThat builds on click and conversion metrics OpenAi has already rolled out--laying the groundwork to turn ChatGPT into a full performance-advertising engine.&nbsp;\nOpenAI also inked deals across Madison Avenue,&nbsp;allowing advertisers to buy ads via Criteo, StackAdapt, and LiveRamp. By May, OpenAI said it would expand its ad pilot to more countries, including U.K, Japan, Brazil, among others.&nbsp;\n“They're not running an ad trial by any discernible metric,” said Elliot. “In an ad trial, you'd be running a small number of these ads to a small number of users, and you would have more leeway to experiment and try new ideas.”&nbsp;\nGrowth is slowing just as OpenAI leans on it\nChatGPT reached 1 billion global app monthly active users in May 2026, according to Sensor Tower estimates, up 63% year-over-year. This comes on the heels of OpenAI touting 900 MAU and 50 million consumer subscribers, as of February.&nbsp;\nBut the growth curve is bending. ChatGPT’s app MAU grew 140% YoY in the first quarter of 2026, sharply down from 362% YoY in the same quarter a year ago.&nbsp;\nStill, ChatGPT remains larger than rivals like Claude (59 million), and Gemini (484 million MAUs), per Sensor Tower data. But the deceleration undercuts a key assumption in OpenAI’s ad forecast: that user growth keeps compounding fast enough to support a rapidly scaling ad business.&nbsp;\nAds run only on Free and Go tier accounts--meaning the platform’s heaviest users don't see them at all, Elliot noted.\n&nbsp;“By definition, the most active users--the people who spend the most time submit the most prompts--won't see ads,” he said. “There are Token limits for usage. If you aren't paying for subs, you aren't using it everyday.”\nSources interviewed for this story said advertisers are treating ChatGPT ads as an experiment budget and not sweating for any meaningful ROI.&nbsp;\nRoy said OpenAI still has a long way to go: build out audience data, negative keyword options, transparent triggering reports like those provided by Google ads.\n“The way that the ChatGPT interface is right now, is it ever going to be as sexy as a Meta ad or Google or PMax?,” Roy said. “Probably not.”&nbsp;",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Premium",
        "Ad Tech Industry News",
        "Artificial Intelligence"
      ],
