    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-07-11-after-years-of-teasing-the-viral-nopia-synth-is-basically-fi",
      "title": "After years of teasing, the viral Nopia synth is ‘basically finished’",
      "summary": "More mint green synths please. | Image: Nopia\t\n\nAfter setting the music gear corner of the internet on fire back in 2023 with the first glimpse at the Nopia, creators Martin Grieco and Rocío Gal are almost ready to bring it to market. The duo brought it to the MusicRadar offices for an in-depth first look and revealed that it will be launching in \"a couple of months\" for around £550.\nNopia is buil",
      "body": "After setting the music gear corner of the internet on fire back in 2023 with the first glimpse at the Nopia, creators Martin Grieco and Rocío Gal are almost ready to bring it to market. The duo brought it to the MusicRadar offices for an in-depth first look and revealed that it will be launching in “a couple of months” for around £550.\nNopia is built around harmonic interplay in a unique way. Rather than a few knobs and a keyboard controlling a single synth patch, it blends multiple modules — keys, bass, arp, and pad — into a single performance, not unlike a drumless groovebox. There’s a one-octave keyboard called the Chord Builder, a 12-button Tonal Selector, and an Extensions Dial that dictate the key and voicing of the chords. The idea is to let you play complex harmonies with just a finger or two.\nAdditional performance features include a strum plate in the top-right corner for plucking specific notes from a chord and a slider for full chord pitch bends.\nIn addition to the virtual analog and sample-based synth engines, there are basic effects like delay, reverb, tape emulation, and beat repeat, as well as a ton of connectivity options, including per-module MIDI output for controlling other instruments with Nopia’s harmonic engine.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
